WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:03.120
We all love images on the web.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.700
Images can be beautiful,
fun, and informative.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:09.990
Now the web started life as a,
a text only medium.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:13.285
Check out these early versions
of well known websites.

00:00:13.285 --> 00:00:15.710
Thefacebook.com looks pretty cool, huh?

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:19.310
But the ability to embed
images was the innovation that

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:21.460
really made the web take off.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:24.980
And now we have an incredible
wealth of images on the web.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:28.700
Here's one of millions of pictures
from the Google Art Project.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.550
You can probably get
closer to the picture here

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:33.050
than you can in the actual museum.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:39.240
Now, given the huge and increasing
variety of devices and contexts for

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:44.370
consuming web content now, we need to
think again about how we use images.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:49.070
I'm Sam Dutton,
a developer advocate at Google.

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:52.000
&gt;&gt; And I'm Cameron Pittman,
a course developer at Udacity.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.130
&gt;&gt; And we'll be teaching you how
to use images responsively so

00:00:55.130 --> 00:01:00.010
they work really well across a variety
of devices, display sizes, and

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:01.790
connectivity scenarios.

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:04.690
&gt;&gt; Throughout this class you'll
be building a webpage design for

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:07.740
a blog site that uses
several kinds of images.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:10.300
&gt;&gt; And by the end you'll be
confident that you can incorporate

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.820
images responsively in your own work.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:13.600
&gt;&gt; Let's get started.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:14.190
&gt;&gt; Yeah, let's do it.

